.TH TESTING 9
.SH NAME
testing \- the self-test discipline
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B "tally :: proc(t: ^Tally, ok: bool, what: string) -> bool"
.B "passed :: proc(t: Tally) -> bool"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Vectra has no host-side test build.
Every subsystem proves itself on the machine that runs it, during boot, and
reports one line.
A self-test on real hardware against real page tables finds a different
class of bug than one on a workstation against a mock, and this tree keeps
hitting the first class.
.I Testing
states the rules a check follows.
See
.IR verify (8)
for the boot-time run and the log a developer reads.
.SS Writing a check
A check is one assertion, counted with
.IR tally (2):
.BI "tally(&r.tally, " ok ", " "\(dqwhat was claimed\(dq" ) .
.I Tally
counts every call, remembers the name given to the first failure, and keeps
up to eight later failures beside it as a trace.
.I Passed
reports true only when every check ran and none failed.
Zero checks is not a pass:
a self-test that returns before it tests anything has nothing to say, and a
run that reports zero checks passed looks exactly like a run with nothing
to do.
.PP
A subsystem's self-test reports one line, tagged
.B "  ok  "
or
.BR " FAIL " .
A failing line names the first check that failed, because later failures
are almost always its consequences.
Up to eight of the later failures print beneath it as
.B also:
lines, and a count of how many more if there were.
The first name is still the one to read.
.SS A check must be able to fail
A green line proves nothing by itself.
It can mean the code is right, or it can mean the check never reaches the
code, or it can mean a layer underneath forgives the very error the check
looks for.
A check earns its place by having a mutation that makes it fail.
Apply the mutation by hand, one change at a time, and record in a comment
beside the check which mutations it catches and which it does not.
An uncaught mutation is not a gap to hide.
It is a fact about the check, recorded next to it.
.PP
A check that always passes is common in three shapes, and each has a fix.
A field the code under test also maintains agrees with itself whatever else
is broken, so a check reads the effect instead: not a byte counter but the
screen it should have drawn to, not a cursor column but the pixels in the
cell.
A layer underneath the check can absorb the very error under test, such as
an allocator that silently ignores a double free, so the layer counts the
event instead of staying silent, and the check brackets the count.
A test can fail to exercise the code at all, such as every sample coming
from window zero where an added offset is indistinguishable from none, so
the check is widened until it does.
.SS Bracketing
A check that measures a resource must bracket every allocator the thing
under test uses, not only the obvious one.
A process holds physical frames and heap objects at once, and a leak of one
without the other passes a check that only counts frames.
A bracket that can go negative is not measuring what it claims to: an
opening count taken before earlier self-tests' leftover threads are reaped
counts objects the closing count does not.
.SS Measuring concurrency
A concurrency check that measures work in a fixed round count measures less
of the machine as the machine gets faster, because an optimized build does
the same work in fewer ticks and draws fewer preemptions.
Measure in ticks instead, for a fixed span of wall-clock scheduling, so a
faster build gets more work done between the same number of interruptions
rather than a smaller one.
.SS A blocking check never runs on the thread that reports
A read, a wait, or any call that can park must not run on the thread whose
line the boot log is waiting to print.
A call that never returns then prints nothing, from that point on, in the
place hardest to attach a debugger to.
Spawn the blocking call on its own thread and watch it from the reporting
thread with a bound.
The bound must be measured in something the failure itself cannot stop:
a tick count is not safe against a check whose failure is a missing
end-of-interrupt, because that failure also stops the timer the bound
counts.
A yield count is safe, because a yield is a software interrupt that
executes rather than arrives.
Where the failure prevents the observer from running at all, such as a
program that enters with interrupts masked, the bound belongs inside the
thing under test instead of in the observer: the test ends itself after a
fixed number of iterations, whatever the kernel does or does not do about
it, and a run that reaches the end on its own is the failure.
.SS A control that can stay
Most mutations are applied once by hand and recorded in prose, which decays
because nothing re-runs them.
Where the wrong arrangement can be expressed as a flag rather than a
deleted line of kernel code, write it as a permanent second path and check
that the wrong path visibly fails where the right one does not.
A failure to fail is then a check every boot runs by itself, with nothing
to revert and nothing to remember.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B kernel/verify_*.odin
subsystem self-tests that need more than one package
.TP
.BI kernel/ pkg /verify.odin
a package's own self-test
.TP
.B sys/vectra9/verify.odin
the layer under the kernel, tested separately because it runs before the
kernel exists
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libodin/tally.odin
.br
.B kernel/log.odin
.br
.B kernel/main.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR tally (2),
.IR verify (8),
.IR style (9)
.SH BUGS
There is no host-side test build.
Every check runs only on the target machine, at boot.
